[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 14:05] Understood. Now the pricing math — walk me through it once more.

[candidate · 15:35] Sure. Current state, revenue = 115.

Costs in dollars: COGS es cincuenta y ocho por ciento de ciento quince = 66.7. Labor, veintidós por ciento = 25.3. Overhead, catorce por ciento = 16.1. Total = 108.1. Profit = 115 − 108.1 = 6.9. Ties to the 6% margin.

Now a 10% price increase, holding volume flat. Revenue = 115 × 1.10 = 126.5. Key point: costs don't move, because COGS and labor scale with cups served, not with price — the same units cost the same to make. So costs stay 108.1.

Profit = 126.5 − 108.1 = 18.4. Margen = 18.4 / 126.5 = 14.5%.

For the full 24%: 0.76R = 108.1, entonces R = 142.2, a 23.7% increase. Too aggressive alone.

The real caveat is my flat-volume assumption. At 10%, some traffic walks. If elasticity costs us 5% of volume, COGS drops proportionally too, so it partially self-corrects — I'd want elasticity data before committing.
