[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 15:37] Beyond price, what else could the client do to reverse the margin decline? Give me a structured set of levers.

[candidate · 17:07] Three buckets, ordered by where the money is.

First, COGS, since that's 89% of the problem. Procurement: lock in forward contracts or hedge green coffee rather than buying spot — they clearly ate the spike unhedged. Diversify origins if they're concentrated in one region. Renegotiate dairy and packaging, where they're paying 37.5% more with 200 stores of buying power they may not be using. And reduce waste — spoilage and over-pouring sit in that line.

Second, mix and revenue quality. Push high-margin items: brewed coffee over dairy-heavy drinks, food attach, loyalty subscriptions. Entonces, si el margen por bebida sube, no necesito tanto precio.

Third, the smaller lines. Overhead crept two points on a flat footprint — that's worth auditing. Labor held flat at 22% while volume grew 15%, so they're already running efficiently; I'd be careful cutting there, since service quality is what supports pricing power.

I'd sequence price and procurement first — fastest, biggest.
