[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 17:10] What's your recommendation to the CEO?

[candidate · 18:40] My recommendation: raise menu prices around 10% and hedge green coffee — that combination recovers most of the margin, and the case for it is that this is a cost problem, not a demand problem.

Supporting points. Sixteen of the eighteen points of margin loss are COGS, and within that, beans more than doubled — de 10.5 a 23.4 en dólares, from 25% to 35% of COGS. Meanwhile prices haven't moved in two years while same-store revenue grew 15% on a flat 200-store footprint. That's real pricing power they haven't used. A 10% increase takes margin from 6% to roughly 14.5%; procurement savings and mix shift toward brewed coffee close more of the gap.

Risks: elasticity is unknown, so I'd phase the increase and test by market; competitors may not follow; hedging locks you in if beans fall.

Next steps: price elasticity test in two or three markets, a bean sourcing and hedging review, and an overhead audit on those two creeping points.
